AU  - Wang, Changchao; Zhao, Shuai; Lan, Zhouyi; Huang, Tengchao
TI  - Pixel-level binocular real-time high-precision depth estimation based on epipolar geometry constraints and resource-constrained systems
PY  - 2025
AB  - <jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>
                  <jats:p>With the rapid advancement of robotics and expanding application scenarios, robots must perceive environmental depth to adapt to complex environments and perform autonomous tasks. This study proposes a binocular vision-based algorithm for high-precision depth measurement in resource-constrained hardware. An optimization model for depth estimation is established by deriving constraint equations for corresponding pixels from camera imaging principles. These equations are solved via epipolar geometric covariance constraints, transforming depth estimation into an optimal transport problem on manifolds through projection Jacobian determinants while incorporating intrinsic parameter uncertainty. This approach achieves near-optimal solutions with significantly reduced computational load. Rigorous benchmarking against canonical stereo methods—geometric triangulation and Semiglobal Block Matching (SGBM)—demonstrates 1.58 × and 32 × faster frame rates respectively. Accuracy achieves &lt;5 mm error (0.3%) within 2 m and &lt;60 mm error (1.5%) within 5 m, far superior to the traditional sparse algorithms algorithm, with consistent robustness under varying illumination.</jats:p>
