AU  - Singh Bisht, Akshaj; Karthik, R; Charan, Guntupalli Sai; Kandepi, Durjai Sri Sai
TI  - A dual-stream deep learning network integrating Xception and context-aware attention for microorganism classification
PY  - 2026
AB  - <jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>
                  <jats:p>Environmental microorganisms (EMs) are essential to the preservation of many ecological processes. By feeding on decomposed organic matter, organisms such as Paramecium and Euglena play an important role in facilitating decomposition and promoting nutrient recycling within their environments. Additionally, EMs play a critical role in waste treatment, climate regulation, plant growth promotion, and biodegradation. Microscopic image analysis offers a non-invasive and scalable method of analyzing EMs. However, the classification of the images poses a challenge, considering the similarity between the shapes of various kinds and the subtle changes in the shapes of the same class of EMs. Moreover, the availability of the data for analysis is limited. Traditional approaches, such as molecular DNA/RNA analysis, physical spectrum analysis, and chemical component analysis, are often expensive and time-consuming. It is therefore imperative to develop an automated method for precisely analyzing and categorizing microbial images. This study presents a new dual-track architecture for EM classification that uses the context-aware efficient attention network (CAEAN) and the state-of-the-art Xception model. The Xception network extracts high-level contextual features from the input images using depth-wise separable convolutions and identity residual connections, enabling effective representation of broader morphological characteristics and helping address inter-species structural similarity at a coarse scale. The CAEAN track, which is designed for smaller-scale datasets, concurrently combines dynamic convolution and the FlexiDeform block to enhance the extraction of fine-grained spatial features, specifically targeting subtle textural and boundary variations that distinguish visually similar species. The complementary representations from both branches are then integrated through the dynamic feature combination module. The proposed network is thus designed to capture a comprehensive set of features, encompassing both contextual and detailed morphological information, while maintaining robustness for smaller-sized datasets. The effectiveness of the proposed model is demonstrated by achieving an accuracy of 74.40% on the EMDS-6 dataset.</jats:p>
